  "name": "catalogue read, 64 notes 72.8M (30.3M core, E33t winner)",
  "description": "The E33t winner, catalogue_reuse_n4 (docs/experiments_specs/ahead/E33t_catalogue_read.md). A page writer reads the text in 256-token pages that do not overlap (stride 256) and writes 64 notes per page. Balanced filing puts every page token under exactly one note, at most 16 tokens per note (4 on average). In the reading layer the notes are ONLY an index, like cards in a library catalogue: a query scores every earlier note (the best head's cosine), opens the best notes until 512 tokens are open (at most 128 notes), and reads those exact tokens plus 4 learned empty slots and the last 512 tokens; the notes' own values are never read. The reading layer and one local layer run 4 times in a loop, and every pass may reopen any note (the stage-A fix: forbidding a pass to reopen notes was the main cause of the gap to LSA). An extra index loss (weight 0.1) teaches the scores to predict where the read's attention goes. Trained like every arm of the testbed: 1.5B FineWeb-Edu tokens in 4k rows, then 0.2B LongDocs-lite tokens in 16k rows; GPT-2 tokenizer. Results: at 30M 87.9 % of the window-to-dense gap at 16k (stage B, Odra, seed 1); the same design at 100M (width 896, 3.0B + 0.4B tokens) 86 % of the gap at 16k, 0.02 nats behind the LSA port. Parameters: 72,769,685 in total, 30,328,597 without the word tables (trainer count, scripts/run_e33n.py model_args('catalogue_reuse_n4', 's30', 1472)).",
